From: Lars Wikman <lars@underjord.io>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: unlink the provider's own handle on unregister

__clk_register() links hw->clk into core->clks along with the
consumers' handles (its error path unlinks it again), but
clk_unregister() frees it without unlinking it. As long as no consumer
holds the core that does no harm: the core goes with it. When one does,
the core outlives the handle and its list points into freed memory,
which clk_core_get_boundaries() walks on the next clk_set_rate().

On the watch the codec holds the ADSP's q6afe clocks. Stopping the ADSP
unregisters them, and when it came back the sound card's re-probe set
the codec's mclk rate and oopsed at a list-poisoned address, with
prepare_lock held, which wedged the system.

Signed-off-by: Lars Wikman <lars@underjord.io>
---
--- a/drivers/clk/clk.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/clk.c
@@ -4593,6 +4593,13 @@ void clk_unregister(struct clk *clk)
 	if (clk->core->protect_count)
 		pr_warn("%s: unregistering protected clock: %s\n",
 					__func__, clk->core->name);
+
+	/*
+	 * __clk_register() linked the provider's own handle in with the
+	 * consumers'. A consumer may keep the core alive past this point and
+	 * walk that list, so take the handle off it before freeing it.
+	 */
+	clk_core_unlink_consumer(clk);
 	clk_prepare_unlock();
 
 	kref_put(&clk->core->ref, __clk_release);
-- 
